            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Most consensus responses agree there is no single settled symbolic meaning for 'year.' They primarily highlight its use as an appointed era of salvation or favor (e.g., the Jubilee or 'acceptable year of the Lord'), while listing its scalable chronological uses (the day-year principle, 1,260-day apocalyptic periods, and 1,000 years as a day) as secondary prophetic alternatives.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines a year strictly as a 'turning' or 'one complete cycle of time,' emphasizing that this cycle is a scalable unit where the count can run at different magnitudes—such as Jeremiah's seventy years being fulfilled as seventy jubilees, or a thousand years passing as a day.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A divinely appointed era (often of favor, salvation, or judgment) or a prophetic unit of time.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"One complete cycle of time that operates at variable, scalable magnitudes.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus treats the soteriological 'Year of the Lord's favor' (Jubilee/salvation era) as the dominant symbolic meaning, with chronological scaling treated as specialized prophetic alternatives. The book bypasses the 'era of favor' motif entirely, elevating scalable chronological cycles as the singular, overarching definition of the symbol.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book's definition is compatible with the prophetic timeframes acknowledged by the consensus, but it synthesizes them under a single root definition ('a complete cycle at whatever scale') rather than treating them as separate interpretive traditions.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both recognize that 'year' is often used figuratively to denote something other than a literal 365-day period, particularly in prophetic texts where time scales. The book refines this by making the scalability of a 'complete cycle' the exclusive core meaning, omitting the consensus's primary focus on the messianic 'era of grace.'\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By focusing entirely on temporal scaling, the book ignores Isaiah 61 and Luke 4 ('the acceptable year of the Lord'), which most interpreters consider the most prominent and central non-literal use of 'year' in the Bible.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
